{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9eb868bbd646f17e","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9013: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\tStrategy priority: Critical. Two affected components (KServe, Platform Operator),\n13\tfour HLRs (P0/P0/P1/P2). Not triaged: the scope spans multiple components and\n14\tmultiple distinct work streams (code remediation in two components, compliance\n15\tevidence assembly and certification submission, documentation authoring), so the\n16\tbelow-threshold and docs-only checks do not apply.\n17\t\n18\t## Epic List\n19\t\n20\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n21\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n22\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | FIPS 140-3 crypto remediation of KServe serving-stack images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS 140-3 crypto remediation of Platform Operator images | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | P0 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package and certification submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | Document the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Docs | P0 |\n26\t\n27\t## Dependency DAG\n28\t\n29\t```mermaid\n30\tgraph TD\n31\t    E001[\"E001 KServe image FIPS remediation\"] --> E003[\"E003 compliance evidence package\"]\n32\t    E002[\"E002 Platform Operator image FIPS remediation\"] --> E003\n33\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 FIPS documentation\"]\n34\t    E002 --> E004\n35\t    E003 --> E004\n36\t```\n37\t\n38\tE001 and E002 are parallel roots (different components, different repos, no\n39\tshared artifacts). E003 consumes both remediation efforts' deliverables. E004 is\n40\tlast (docs-authoring, blocked by every Implementation epic).\n41\t\n42\t## DAG Justification\n43\t\n44\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n45\t|------|------|-----------|\n46\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 14 | Different components and repos with no shared artifacts — the two image remediation efforts run in parallel. |\n47\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (producer → consumer) | The evidence package's content — module inventory, build attestations, and FIPS test results for the KServe images — is produced by the KServe remediation work; the package cannot be assembled or submitted before those deliverables exist. This is a deliverable-content dependency on general Implementations, not a build-pipeline output consumed by a konflux-onboarding epic (no Rule 10 concern arises). |\n48\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (producer → consumer) | Same relationship for the Platform Operator images: their inventory entries, attestations, and test results are inputs to the evidence package. |\n49\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n50\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n51\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n52\t\n53\tNo Investigation epics. The strategy states \"None on scope\" for open\n54\tquestions. The one systematic unknown — which specific images link\n55\tnon-validated crypto — does not change which epics exist or what they do: the\n56\tremediation epics audit, remediate, and verify the same image set either way,\n57\thandling findings fix-and-retry, and the resulting module inventory is a\n58\tdeliverable of that work (feeding the evidence package). Lab scheduling and\n59\treview-board cadence are externally controlled schedule constraints, recorded as\n60\trisks on E003 — not unknowns that gate epic structure, and deliberately not\n61\tacceptance criteria.\n62\t\n63\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n64\t\n65\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n66\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n67\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E002 | Split by component (KServe images vs Platform Operator images), not by deferral — both halves are required for \"all serving-stack images\". |\n68\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E003 | Evidence package assembly, security review board sign-off, and certification lab submission. |\n69\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E001, E002 (bundled) | The CI FIPS-regression gate is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable: each remediation epic owns the gating check for exactly the image surfaces and build pipeline it converts, with the corresponding AC. Not separately schedulable — a standalone CI epic would rebuild the same detection scan over the same surfaces and leave each remediation epic's guardrail outside its scope (work-type split; contract check 6). |\n70\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E004 | Substantive new documentation topics (support posture + cluster prerequisites) — a distinct content-authoring work stream, warranting a separate docs-authoring epic. |\n71\t\n72\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n73\t\n74\t## Health Warnings\n75\t\n76\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n77\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, and no\n78\t  Non-Functional Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded from the HLRs\n79\t  and Affected Components; epic acceptance criteria are derived from the HLRs.\n80\t- **Team assumptions**: KServe epics are assigned to RHOAI Serving (per the\n81\t  architecture context file). The strategy names no teams, so the Platform\n82\t  Operator epic is assigned to RHOAI Platform and the documentation epic to\n83\t  RHOAI Docs as assumptions — verify with the engagement owners before\n84\t  scheduling.\n85\t- **Platform Operator has no architecture-context file** in this strategy's\n86\t  context fetch. It is treated as in-platform (it is in the canonical RHAI Jira\n87\t  component list and the strategy's premise is existing operator images linking\n88\t  crypto libraries), so no onboarding chain was generated; its\n89\t  `architecture_claims` signal is scored 0 for lack of a corroborating file.\n90\t- **\"Serving data plane\" scope ambiguity**: Affected Components lists only\n91\t  KServe and Platform Operator, so the serving data plane is decomposed as\n92\t  KServe's serving images. If Serving Runtimes images (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit)\n93\t  were intended to be in scope, additional epics under that component would be\n94\t  required — confirm with the strategy author.\n95\t- **Docs epic priority**: E004 is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception\n96\t  (strategy priority Critical → P0) even though HLR-4 is P2. This is by design,\n97\t  not a priority inversion; E004 does not push priority onto its dependencies.\n98\t- **Epic size**: E001 and E002 may exceed the ~2-week guideline — each is one\n99\t  logical sweep (audit → remediate → verify → wire CI gate) over its\n100\t  component's image set, and splitting would duplicate the audit/detection\n101\t  mechanism across epics. Conditional re-split trigger: re-split by sub-system\n102\t  (control-plane images vs data-plane serving images) if the crypto audit finds\n103\t  materially distinct remediation approaches per image group (for example Go\n104\t  toolchain rebuilds vs Python dependency swaps) rather than the single pattern\n105\t  the strategy's \"several images\" phrasing implies.\n106\t- **External schedule risk** (recorded as risk, not AC, per the AC rules):\n107\t  certification lab turnaround is historically six to nine months and\n108\t  review-board cadence is fixed — both externally controlled and dominant over\n109\t  the schedule. E003's acceptance criteria stop at the engineering completion\n110\t  point the team controls: package assembled, board-approved, submitted, and\n111\t  triaged by the lab.\n112\t\n113\t## Component Mapping Notes\n114\t\n115\t- E003's work spans KServe and Platform Operator (the evidence package covers\n116\t  all serving-stack images); it is assigned the single most-relevant canonical\n117\t  component, **KServe**, as the serving-stack centerpiece.\n118\t- E004 uses the canonical **Documentation** component (docs-authoring work).\n119\t- E001 → KServe and E002 → Platform Operator map verbatim from the canonical\n120\t  list; no non-obvious mappings beyond the two above.\n121\t\n122\t## Tiered Delivery\n123\t\n124\t- **Tier 1 (independent value, not gated by the certification outcome)**:\n125\t  E001 and E002 — serving-stack images run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters\n126\t  (HLR-1) and CI guards against regressions (HLR-3). This is deliverable and\n127\t  valuable to public-sector deployments before any certification completes.\n128\t- **Tier 2 (externally gated)**: E003 — the certification evidence submission\n129\t  (HLR-2). Its completion timing is dominated by the accredited laboratory and\n130\t  the security review board, not by engineering speed. No upstream-PR epic is\n131\t  warranted (the strategy makes no code contribution to a third party); the lab\n132\t  is a process gate on E003's own deliverable. E004 follows the certification\n133\t  submission so the documented posture reflects the submitted evidence.\n134\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bfb40571d500dae6","agent_id":"adf1730c530c0209a","session_id":"177f2182-dcad-4f2f-b1fd-9c1f9eb2a18a","uuid":"2589117e-e862-44ed-9565-d89c4a5cbe1d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:21:54.213Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9eb868bbd646f17e","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9013 decomposition"}
